WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.334 --> 00:00:01.167
- Back off.

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.120
- [Shelby] In the high profile trial

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:04.010
of Theranos founder, Elizabeth Holmes,

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:06.220
prosecutors have been
trying to prove that Holmes

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:09.100
intentionally defrauded
patients and investors

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:10.350
by making false claims

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:12.800
about her company's
blood testing technology.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:14.740
Holmes has pleaded not guilty.

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:17.600
- [Elizabeth] This company
is about being able

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.570
to change the healthcare industry.

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:21.910
- As prosecutors continue
to make their case,

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:24.200
speculation has grown about whether Holmes

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:25.750
will take the stand.

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:27.890
- The witnesses we've heard from have said

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:29.300
how they were totally enamored by her.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:30.250
They totally believed her.

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:32.950
She had presence that controlled the room.

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:34.780
- [Shelby] To catch you
up on three key pieces

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:37.040
of evidence presented at trial so far,

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.560
and a look at what's to come,

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.120
we talked to Wall Street Journal reporter,

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:44.090
Sara Randazzo, who's been
covering the trial in California.

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:46.400
First, audio recordings have given jurors

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.540
the chance to hear Holmes pitch Theraons

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.970
to investors in 2013.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:54.280
- [Elizabeth] The ability to
take a technology like this

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:58.380
and put it in place,
specifically on a medivac,

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:01.340
has the potential to
change survival rates.

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:03.270
- We're hearing Elizabeth Holmes touting

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:05.680
how they can do all of these
things with the machines,

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:06.990
how they're working with the military

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:08.250
and the military is huge for them.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.030
And so all of these
things that at this point,

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:13.030
the jurors have heard, it
contained a lot of falsehoods

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:14.590
and weren't quite true.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:17.240
And so now they're hearing
Elizabeth Holmes' state directly,

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:18.430
which is pretty powerful.

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:20.470
- Some investors that said Holmes' promise

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:22.760
to help members of the
military compelled them

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:24.290
to invest in the company.

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:26.410
In cross-examination, attorneys for Holmes

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:28.290
have made the case that Theranos investors

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.760
should have asked questions
if they had concerns.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:32.090
And that they knew investing

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:33.610
in a new type of technology,

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:36.870
like the kind of Theranos was
developing, came with risks.

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:39.180
Prosecutors also read
private text messages

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:41.050
between Holmes and her former boyfriend,

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:43.660
Sunny Balwani, who was the
number two at the company.

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:46.450
- There's a lot of texts
showing Sunny and Elizabeth

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:48.380
texting about concerns or things

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:49.630
that they're worried about.

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:52.610
- In one 2015 message,
following media appearances

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:54.827
made by Holmes, Balwani wrote,

00:01:54.827 --> 00:01:57.220
"FYI, I am worried about overexposure

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:00.150
without solid substance,
which is lacking right now."

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:02.080
Holmes responded that the media coverage

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.170
helped Theranos get a business deal.

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:06.260
- It's important because
a big part of this trial

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:08.100
is showing Elizabeth Holmes' intent

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:09.680
and that she intended to deceive.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:11.560
So if jurors can come to believe

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.250
that she thought there
were a bunch of concerns,

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:15.640
but then publicly was
saying nothing was wrong,

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:18.010
that can help them in their deliberations.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:19.440
- [Shelby] The defense
has argued that Holmes

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:21.650
relied on Balwani for accurate information

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:23.340
about the company and that she believes

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.510
she was telling the truth about Theranos.

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:27.850
Balwani, who faces his own separate trial,

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:30.010
has denied any wrong doing.

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:32.820
Another key piece of evidence
shown by the prosecution,

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:35.150
an allegedly forged endorsement.

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:37.560
This document, emblazoned
with the Pfizer logo,

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.720
purported to show that
Theranos' technology

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:42.100
had been validated by
the pharmaceutical giant,

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:45.390
but prosecutors say it was
used to deceive investors.

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:47.940
- What's come out is that
they did work with Pfizer

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:49.980
in the very early years of the company.

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.050
Pfizer then had a scientist, Shane Weber,

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:54.650
analyze everything that they'd seen

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:57.270
and internally write a
report that concluded

00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:58.970
they didn't want to
pursue the relationship.

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:00.890
- [Shelby] Weber said
he himself told Holmes

00:03:00.890 --> 00:03:03.440
that Pfizer wasn't interested
in working with Theranos.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:04.580
But about a year later,

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:07.540
Theranos put out a report
with the Pfizer logo on it,

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:09.730
which Holmes use to pitch to investors.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:11.940
Some investors testified
that they took the report

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:14.480
at face value and that it
played into their decision

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:16.960
to invest many millions of dollars.

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:18.980
Of course, there's been
plenty of other evidence

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:20.380
presented at trial so far,

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:23.216
which you can read more about at wsj.com.

00:03:23.216 --> 00:03:24.049
(soft music)

00:03:24.049 --> 00:03:26.720
So what's next in the
closely watched trial?

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:29.060
The defense will soon have
a chance to make its case,

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:30.920
and it could go a few different ways.

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:32.830
As lawyer suggested in a court filing,

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:35.300
they could argue that Holmes
was in an abusive relationship

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.820
with Balwani that left
her under his control.

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:41.390
A lawyer for Balwani disputed
any allegations of abuse.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:43.820
Or as the defense argued
in opening statements,

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:45.720
it could continue making
the case that Holmes

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:47.510
simply had a startup that failed,

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:49.750
like many others in Silicon Valley.

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:51.410
The defense could also simply rest

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:53.790
hoping that prosecutors
didn't prove Holmes' guilt

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:55.260
beyond a reasonable doubt.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:58.290
- I think it's gonna
be a bit of a surprise

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:00.950
and shock moment in court
when they stand up and say,

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:03.940
whether they're calling someone
or just resting the case.

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:06.430
- Holmes is facing 12 counts of wire fraud

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:08.430
and conspiracy to commit wire fraud

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:09.880
with the top charges carrying

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:13.053
a maximum federal prison
sentence of 20 years.

